A||||7||Mark Matthews||Final Tests before getting Fiero||||14||
Z||000000||Mark Matthews||12-05-2001||12:52 PM||||Just a couple question before I purchase my first Fiero.  BAM-BAM helped me out last night on the phone with stuff to check for.  I'm wondering what other people want to add to my list.<P>• I'm bringing my compression tester, so I'd like the numbers for an '87 GT V6 to compare against.  Engine is bone stock, I believe!<P>• Printed the list of ECM Codes, so I'll check those.  Some have said it's a torque screw on the plate, and then I also heard allen screws.  I don't know if I have a torque screwdriver... what's that?<P>• I was gonna check the brake pads, what's the minimum depth on a pad before replacing?<P>• What's the easiest way to check out the shocks?  Aren't they hidden behind a liner?  I don't wanna get it and find what TRiAD found!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>• What else am I missing?<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||14
Z||000001||cadaver||12-05-2001||01:02 PM||||The numbers on my v6 last time I did them were around 90. Its not the number that is that important it how close all the numbers are to each other.<P>Yes it is a torx. Number 15. A torx is like an allen but it is star shapped. Has points instead of flats. Bits are available at any tool or hardware store. Pretty common now days.<P>If it needs pads thats no biggie. Look at the rotors for grooves. Even then its just buying rotors if needed.<P>No the shock are not behind the liners. Only the front has them. The back are struts.<BR>And again, if it needs shock then it needs shocks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||12-05-2001||01:03 PM||||Well, your list is pretty good (I usually wouldn't think to compression test...that's a good one!)<P><BR>[EDIT]He beat to the T15 thing... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">[/EDIT]<BR>As for the torque/allen screws.  It is a T15 Torque screw (same for all interior gauge panel covers- and many exterior trim parts as well).  A Torque bit looks like a start tip with 8(?) points.  Stop by sears- get a T15 screwdriver or rachet style socket (I prefer the screwdriver-style for interior work).<P><B>Rust Check!</B><BR>pull back the trunk carpet and look for rust under there... also on the K-frame of the engine itself.<P>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero_Freak||12-05-2001||01:09 PM||||   Don't forget the all important rust.  Look up under the front and check the radiator support and cross bracing.  I noticed these seem to be the first to go. Also pull the trunk carpet on both sides and look at the outer wall.  See if there are rust holes there.  Found one as big as my head one time! <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg">  <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||11
Z||000004||85 gt fiero||12-05-2001||01:09 PM||||the compression shouldnt be less than 100 psi, warm the car up for about 5 minutes then shut it off and let it sit for 5 minutes then testcompression, but remove the air cleaner housing if possible, and make sure the battery is fully charged, if the compression is over 135 you have carbon buildup in the chambers and the head need to be decarbonated.  randy hope this helps and anyone correct me if wrong.<P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark Matthews||12-05-2001||02:25 PM||||Thanks guys!  I just noticed something tho.  The one I'm looking at looks basically like Fiero-Freak's!  Same color two-tone, fastback, Ttop (instead of sunroof), and no wing on back.  Nice car!<P>About doing the Jalapeno to it, we'll see.  If I like enough as it is, I may just have to get another to do the kit to!  BAM-BAM warned me to watch my back if you guys heard I turned it into a Jalapeno!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||1
Z||000006||85 gt fiero||12-05-2001||02:28 PM||||Jalapeno?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> that doesnt even sound good in my opinion or even look good, but if it floats your boat go for it.<P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||1
Z||000007||West Coast Fiero||12-05-2001||02:57 PM||||It may not hurt either to check for crushed, bent, or damaged coolant tubes underneath the car. These are often mangled by repair shops putting these cars on lifts, unknowingly to them that they use the tubes as lift points||||reg||1
